@meta
id: 01-06
title: When a command lands in your file
chapter: 01
type: lesson
skills: mode-awareness, insert-a, esc, survival-motion
par: 18
minutes: 4
vscode: nothing — there is only one mode to be in
vimrc: set backspace=indent,eol,start
open: refund.entity.ts

@brief
Every vim user does this in their first week. You think you are in normal
mode, you are not, and a command you meant to run lands in the file as
text. Someone did exactly that to refund.entity.ts: they pressed `dd`
intending to delete a line, while still in insert mode, and got two
letters.

Nothing is broken. The debris is just characters, and characters come out
the way they went in — from insert mode, with Backspace.

Get rid of the stray `dd` at the end of the `orderId` line. Leave the rest
of the file exactly as it is.

@keys
| `a` | append after the cursor, so Backspace eats what is to the left |
| `Backspace` | in insert mode, delete the character before the cursor |
| `Esc` | out |
| `$` | end of the line |

@hints
The junk is at the end of line three. Get the cursor onto that line and
then to the end of it.
--
If you are sitting on the final character, `a` puts you in insert mode
just past it — which is where Backspace does what you want.
--
`$a` then Backspace twice, then `Esc`, then save and quit.

@solution
    j j $ a <BS> <BS> <Esc> :wq<Enter>

The useful habit here is not the keys, it is the diagnosis. When letters
appear in your file that you did not mean to type, you were in insert
mode. When nothing appears at all and the file starts jumping about, you
were in normal mode. Neither is a fault in the editor and neither is
serious.

Look at the bottom left before you accuse vim of anything. `-- INSERT --`
means your keys are text. A blank there means your keys are commands.

One oddity of the dojo itself: Backspace is a special key, and the
keystroke log records it the way it records an arrow key. This is the one
lesson where the "arrow keys detected" note at the end is lying to you.

Later chapters give you `x` to delete a character from normal mode and `u`
to undo the last change, and both are better tools than Backspace for this
job. Today you only have the modes themselves, and the point is that the
modes are already enough to dig yourself out.

@start refund.entity.ts
export type Refund = {
  id: string;
  orderId: string;dd
  amount: number;
  status: RefundStatus;
};

@expected refund.entity.ts
export type Refund = {
  id: string;
  orderId: string;
  amount: number;
  status: RefundStatus;
};
